---
import CodePanel from '../../components/docs/CodePanel.astro';
import { docsApiBaseUrl } from '../../config/docs';
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const description =
  'Test writes, validation errors, rate limits, loading states, and intentional API failures with ApiMocker.';
const createTodo = `curl -X POST '${docsApiBaseUrl}/todos' \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"title":"Test the success state","completed":false,"userId":1}'`;
const validationError = `{
  "error": "Validation Error",
  "message": "Invalid input data",
  "details": [
    {
      "type": "field",
      "value": "",
      "msg": "Title is required and must be between 1 and 200 characters",
      "path": "title",
      "location": "body"
    }
  ]
}`;
const generalLimitError = `{
  "error": "Too Many Requests",
  "message": "Too many requests from this IP, please try again later."
}`;
const writeLimitError = `{
  "error": "Too Many Requests",
  "message": "Write limit exceeded. Maximum 100 write operations per day per IP.",
  "resetTime": "2026-07-27T14:30:00.000Z"
}`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Errors and testing" description={description}>
  <header class="docs-hero docs-hero-compact">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Testing guide</p>
    <h1>Exercise more than the happy path.</h1>
    <p>
      Create temporary records, update state, delete data, slow responses, and trigger known
      failures without setting up a backend.
    </p>
  </header>

  <aside class="docs-callout docs-callout-warning">
    <strong>Writes are public</strong>
    <p>
      The free dataset is shared. Do not send secrets or personal information. All records reset
      to the original seed data at midnight UTC.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section class="docs-section" id="writes">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Mutations</p>
    <h2>Test create, update, and delete flows</h2>
    <p>
      Send JSON to POST, PUT, and PATCH endpoints. A successful create returns 201, updates return
      200, and a successful delete returns 204 with no response body.
    </p>
    <CodePanel code={createTodo} id="testing-create" label="Create a todo" language="bash" />
    <div class="testing-grid">
      <article><code>POST</code><h3>Create</h3><p>Add a temporary record. Server-generated IDs and timestamps are returned.</p></article>
      <article><code>PUT</code><h3>Replace</h3><p>Send every required field for the resource.</p></article>
      <article><code>PATCH</code><h3>Update</h3><p>Send only the fields that should change.</p></article>
      <article><code>DELETE</code><h3>Remove</h3><p>Delete a record until the next daily reset.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="validation">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Validation</p>
    <h2>Handle invalid input</h2>
    <p>
      Missing fields, invalid emails, out-of-range strings, and invalid IDs return a 400 response.
      Validation responses include field-level details when available.
    </p>
    <CodePanel code={validationError} id="testing-validation" label="400 response" language="json" />
    <aside class="docs-note">
      Database conflicts and unexpected failures can use a different JSON shape. Always use the
      HTTP status and display the returned <code>message</code> or <code>error</code> value.
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="error-routes">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Error simulation</p>
    <h2>Trigger predictable failures</h2>
    <div class="error-route-list">
      <a href={`${docsApiBaseUrl}/error/404`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span><code>GET /error/404</code><strong>Not found</strong></span><b>404</b></a>
      <a href={`${docsApiBaseUrl}/error/500`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span><code>GET /error/500</code><strong>Server failure</strong></span><b>500</b></a>
      <a href={`${docsApiBaseUrl}/error/validation`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span><code>GET /error/validation</code><strong>Validation failure</strong></span><b>400</b></a>
      <div><span><code>GET /error/timeout</code><strong>Request that never completes</strong></span><b>Timeout</b></div>
    </div>
    <aside class="docs-note docs-note-danger">
      The timeout route intentionally does not respond. Test it with an abort signal or a client
      timeout so your request can be cancelled.
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="limits">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Limits and reset</p>
    <h2>Design around the shared service</h2>
    <div class="limit-grid">
      <article><strong>1,000 requests</strong><p>Maximum requests per IP address in a rolling 15-minute window. Every public API method counts toward this quota.</p></article>
      <article><strong>100 writes</strong><p>Additional combined POST, PUT, PATCH, and DELETE limit per IP address in a rolling 24-hour window.</p></article>
      <article><strong>Midnight UTC</strong><p>The shared database returns to its original users, posts, todos, and comments.</p></article>
      <article><strong>Open CORS</strong><p>Browser requests are accepted from any origin, and total-count headers are exposed.</p></article>
      <article><strong>10 MB JSON</strong><p>JSON request bodies larger than 10 MB are rejected.</p></article>
    </div>
    <aside class="docs-note">
      <strong>Two separate windows</strong>
      <p>
        Writes count toward both quotas. Both counters are held by the API process and are not
        shared across multiple instances. Restarting the service starts new windows. The midnight
        database reset is separate and does not reset either running-process counter.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="rate-limit-responses">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Rate-limit responses</p>
    <h2>Handle 429 responses explicitly</h2>
    <p>
      The general limiter returns standard <code>RateLimit-Policy</code>,
      <code>RateLimit-Limit</code>, <code>RateLimit-Remaining</code>, and
      <code>RateLimit-Reset</code> headers. The reset value is the number of seconds remaining in
      the rolling 15-minute window. A general-limit 429 also includes <code>Retry-After</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="example-stack">
      <CodePanel code={generalLimitError} id="testing-general-limit" label="General limit 429" language="json" />
      <CodePanel code={writeLimitError} id="testing-write-limit" label="Write limit 429" language="json" />
    </div>
    <aside class="docs-note">
      <p>
        The standard headers describe the 1,000-request general quota. Server clients and API
        tools can read them directly, but the current CORS configuration does not expose them to
        browser JavaScript. The additional write limiter reports its reset time in the JSON body
        rather than separate quota headers. Browser clients should always handle the 429 status.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
